"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkaminsaied||=[]).push([[130],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"lean-formal-proofs","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/lean-formal-proofs","source":"@site/blog/2025-12-02-lean-formal-proofs/index.md","title":"Lean, formal proofs, and verifiable reasoning","description":"An introduction to Lean, its mathematical history, and how formal proofs help us distinguish correct reasoning from plausible answers.","date":"2025-12-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Lean","permalink":"/blog/tags/lean"},{"inline":false,"label":"Reinforcement learning","permalink":"/blog/tags/reinforcement-learning"}],"readingTime":21.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Amin Saied","title":"Member of Technical Staff, Microsoft AI","url":"https://github.com/aminsaied","imageURL":"https://github.com/aminsaied.png","key":"aminsaied","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Lean, formal proofs, and verifiable reasoning","description":"An introduction to Lean, its mathematical history, and how formal proofs help us distinguish correct reasoning from plausible answers.","slug":"lean-formal-proofs","authors":["aminsaied"],"tags":["lean","reinforcement-learning"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Composition factors and representation stability","permalink":"/blog/composition-factors"}},"content":"import {ConstraintFigure, ProofWalkthrough, TacticRow, TacticTable} from \'@site/src/components/LeanArticle\';\\n\\nLean is a programming language in which mathematical proofs can be checked by a computer. This post gives an introduction to Lean, from its origins and recent history to its applications to model reasoning and verifiable reinforcement learning.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What is Lean?\\n\\n[Lean][lean-docs] is a modern interactive _theorem prover_ and functional programming language. It takes this dual nature seriously, and can be used meaningfully for both tasks.\\n\\n**Functional programming language.** As a language, Lean is a strict, pure functional language with a dependent type theory. The latter property here is the most important to understand: it means that types in Lean can contain programs, and that programs can compute types. It is this powerful type system that enables Lean to \\"do mathematics\\".\\n\\n```text\\n# regular types - notice these only depend on other types\\nList Int\\nProd String Bool\\n\\n# dependent types\\nVector Int 5        -- a list of length 5\\nFin n               -- natural numbers less than n\\nMatrix m n \u211d        -- matrices indexed by m and n, with values in \u211d\\n```\\n\\n**Interactive theorem prover.** Lean is expressive enough to do mathematics in a way that feels natural and idiomatic. For example, consider this definition and statement related to prime numbers:\\n\\n```lean\\n/-- A prime is a number larger than 1 with no nontrivial divisors -/\\ndef IsPrime (n : Nat) := 1 < n \u2227 \u2200 k, 1 < k \u2192 k < n \u2192 \xac k \u2223 n\\n\\n/-- Every number larger than 1 has a prime factor -/\\ntheorem exists_prime_factor :\\n    \u2200 n, 1 < n \u2192 \u2203 k, IsPrime k \u2227 k \u2223 n\\n```\\n\\nA proof of the theorem `exists_prime_factor` is a term of that type. If one is able to produce such a term as validated by Lean\'s kernel, then one has proved the theorem!\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>Continuing this example</summary>\\n\\n```lean\\n/-- A prime is a number larger than 1 with no nontrivial divisors -/\\ndef IsPrime (n : Nat) := 1 < n \u2227 \u2200 k, 1 < k \u2192 k < n \u2192 \xac k \u2223 n\\n\\n/-- Every number larger than 1 has a prime factor -/\\ntheorem exists_prime_factor :\\n    \u2200 n, 1 < n \u2192 \u2203 k, IsPrime k \u2227 k \u2223 n := by\\n  intro n h1\\n  -- Either `n` is prime...\\n  by_cases h_prime : IsPrime n\\n  \xb7 grind [Nat.dvd_refl]\\n  -- ... or it has a non-trivial divisor with a prime factor\\n  \xb7 obtain \u27e8k, hk\u27e9 : \u2203 k, 1 < k \u2227 k < n \u2227 k \u2223 n := by\\n      simp_all [IsPrime]\\n    obtain \u27e8p, hp, hpk\u27e9 := exists_prime_factor k (by grind)\\n    grind [Nat.dvd_trans]\\n\\n/-- The factorial, defined recursively, with custom notation -/\\ndef factorial : Nat \u2192 Nat\\n  | 0 => 1\\n  | n+1 => (n + 1) * factorial n\\nnotation:10000 n \\"!\\" => factorial n\\n\\n/-- The factorial is always positive -/\\ntheorem factorial_pos : \u2200 n, 0 < n ! := by\\n  intro n; induction n <;>\\n    grind [factorial, Nat.mul_pos_iff_of_pos_left]\\n\\n/-- ... and divisible by its constituent factors -/\\ntheorem dvd_factorial : \u2200 n, \u2200 k \u2264 n, 0 < k \u2192 k \u2223 n ! := by\\n  intro n; induction n <;>\\n    grind [Nat.dvd_mul_right, Nat.dvd_mul_left_of_dvd, factorial]\\n\\n/--\\nWe show that we find arbitrarily large (and thus infinitely\\nmany) prime numbers, by picking an arbitrary number `n`\\nand showing that `n! + 1` has a prime factor larger than `n`.\\n-/\\ntheorem InfinitudeOfPrimes : \u2200 n, \u2203 p > n, IsPrime p := by\\n  intro n\\n  have : 1 < n ! + 1 := by grind [factorial_pos]\\n  obtain \u27e8p, hp, _\u27e9 := exists_prime_factor (n ! + 1) this\\n  suffices \xacp \u2264 n by grind\\n  intro (_ : p \u2264 n)\\n  have : 1 < p := hp.1\\n  have : p \u2223 n ! := dvd_factorial n p \u2039p \u2264 n\u203a (by grind)\\n  have := Nat.dvd_sub \u2039p \u2223 n ! + 1\u203a \u2039p \u2223 n !\u203a\\n  grind [Nat.add_sub_cancel_left, Nat.dvd_one]\\n```\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n**Dual nature of Lean.** The way to connect these concepts is as follows. Theorems in Lean (or mathematical statements in general) are thought of as types, and a proof of a theorem corresponds to an instance of that type.\\n\\n```text\\n# as a programming language\\nn : Int := 73\\n\\n# as a theorem prover\\ntheorem exists_prime_factor : \u2200 n, 1 < n \u2192 \u2203 k, IsPrime k \u2227 k \u2223 n := by intro n h1 ...\\n```\\n\\nThis simple example already speaks to the potential of Lean as a way to verify statements of mathematics (and by extension, of reasoning) through a compiler. Before going any deeper however, let\'s start with a bit of historical context.\\n\\n### A brief history\\n\\n**Origins of Lean at Microsoft Research.**\\n\\nLean was created by Leonardo de Moura at Microsoft Research (MSR) in 2013 with the goal of building a next-generation theorem prover. By 2017, Lean 3 was released, which became the first mature and widely adopted version.\\n\\n**Lean enters pure mathematics - AKA the Buzzard effect.**\\n\\nIn 2019, Kevin Buzzard gives a talk at Microsoft on [the Future of Mathematics][future-of-mathematics] arguing that Lean will become an essential tool for working mathematicians. This catalyzes a cultural shift in the mathematics community, exemplified by figures like Peter Scholze and Terry Tao (both winners of the Fields Medal) adopting Lean.\\n\\nThere are two important questions answered in this period:\\n\\n1. Is Lean **_broad_** enough to cover the full spectrum of mathematics - from algebra to number theory, from statistics to theoretical physics?\\n2. Is Lean powerful enough to express the **deepest** areas of mathematics - including the latest research? One might fear that as you approach the frontiers of mathematics research, the objects themselves become prohibitively expensive to formalize.\\n\\nThere is strong evidence supporting positive answers to both questions, which we now go over.\\n\\n**On the question of breadth.**\\n\\n- Formalizing the undergraduate curriculum at Imperial College London: In 2021, Kevin Buzzard started to systematically formalize the pure mathematics curriculum at Imperial College. This program covers topics from algebra, number theory, measure theory, topology, graph theory, Galois theory and commutative algebra ([repo][imperial-math-repo], [blog post][xena-intro]).\\n- [`Mathlib`][mathlib] is the unified mathematics library for Lean. Created in 2017, by November 2025 it contained roughly 1.9 million lines of code, over 240k theorems, and over 100k definitions, covering an incredibly broad range of subjects. You can see its [growth statistics here][mathlib-stats]. Check out these papers from [2019][mathlib-white-paper] and [2025][mathlib-growth-paper] for more details.\\n\\n**Lean demonstrates research-level capability.**\\n\\nIn 2020 Peter Scholze (Fields Medalist) announces the [Liquid Tensor Experiment][liquid-tensor] with the goal to formalize a difficult foundational theorem from his work on analytic geometry. Here is the technical statement they aimed to formalize:\\n\\n> **Theorem [Clausen-Scholze].** Let $0 < p_0 < p \\\\le 1$ be real numbers, let $S$ be a profinite set, and let $V$ be a $p$-Banach space. Let $M_{p_0}(S)$ be the space of $p_0$-measures on $S$. Then\\n>\\n> $$\\n> \\\\operatorname{Ext}^i_{\\\\operatorname{Cond}(\\\\mathbf{Ab})}\\n>    \\\\bigl( M_{p_0}(S),\\\\, V \\\\bigr) = 0\\n> $$\\n>\\n> for all $i \\\\ge 1$.\\n\\nThis is an important example because both the objects being discussed and the theorem itself are highly complex (it requires years of training to even understand the basic definitions). To quote Kevin Buzzard:\\n\\n> Hales and Gonthier have already demonstrated beyond all doubt that it is possible to prove highly complex theorems about relatively low-level objects (spheres, finite groups, planar graphs) in a theorem prover. This is the next step - **can we prove a complex theorem about a complex object?**\\n\\nAround six months later, the key result in the challenge was formalized, and a year later the entire challenge was completed. Here\'s a quote from Peter Scholze\'s [blog post][liquid-6mo] announcing the result:\\n\\n> I am excited to announce that the Experiment has verified the entire part of the argument that I was unsure about. I find it absolutely insane that interactive proof assistants are now at the level that within a very reasonable time span they can formally verify difficult original research. Congratulations to everyone involved in the formalization!!\\n\\nThere\'s a lot more one can say about this experiment! For the curious:\\n\\n- Scholze\'s [challenge in full][liquid-pdf].\\n- Buzzard\'s [Beyond Liquid Tensor Experiment][liquid-beyond] blog post.\\n- An [interactive visualization][liquid-deps] of the completed proof.\\n\\n**TL;DR:** Lean can formalize deep mathematics.\\n\\n**Mathematics community.**\\n\\nLean has become a prominent tool within the mathematics community. Figures like Terry Tao are writing about formalization and discussing [Lean in talks][tao-talk]. Ambitious projects like the [**Fermat\'s Last Theorem formalization project**][flt-docs] (Buzzard et al., estimated to take more than ten years to complete) are receiving grants.\\n\\n**DeepMind reaches silver-medal standard at the IMO (2024).**\\n\\nThe machine learning community has taken notice of Lean too, with so-called _neural theorem provers_ gaining popularity. In 2024, [DeepMind achieved silver-medal-level performance on IMO problems][deepmind]. Here\'s an example problem that AlphaProof was able to solve.\\n\\n> **IMO 2024 (Problem 2).** Determine all pairs $(a, b)$ of positive integers for which there exist positive integers $g$ and $N$ such that\\n>\\n> $$\\n> \\\\gcd(a^n + b, b^n + a) = g\\n> $$\\n>\\n> holds for all integers $n \\\\geq N$.\\n\\nThis can be formalized in Lean as follows.\\n\\n```lean\\ntheorem imo_2024_p2 :\\n    {(a, b) |  0 < a \u2227 0 < b \u2227\\n      \u2203 g N, 0 < g \u2227 0 < N \u2227\\n        \u2200 n \u2265 N, Nat.gcd (a ^ n + b) (b ^ n + a) = g} = {(1, 1)} :=\\n```\\n\\n**Remark.** Notice that the original question is to _find all_ pairs. Here, that task is expressed as a statement that the set of solutions is exactly $\\\\{(1, 1)\\\\}$. Finding the candidate solution and proving that it is complete are separate parts of the task.\\n\\nHere is the [original proof produced by AlphaProof][alpha-proof-imo-24-p2]:\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>AlphaProof full solution to IMO 2024 (Problem 2)</summary>\\n\\n```lean\\nrefine Set.eq_singleton_iff_unique_mem.mpr \u27e8?_, ?_\u27e9\\n  \xb7 simp only [Set.mem_setOf]\\n    use one_pos; use one_pos\\n    use 2; use 1\\n    use two_pos; use one_pos\\n    rintro n -\\n    simp only [one_pow]\\n    exact Nat.gcd_self 2\\n\\n  rintro \u27e8a, b\u27e9\\n  rintro \u27e8a_pos, b_pos, g, N, -, -, D\u27e9\\n\\n  have a_coprime : a.Coprime (1 + a * b) := by\\n    rw [Nat.coprime_add_mul_left_right]\\n    exact Nat.coprime_one_right _\\n  have b_coprime : b.Coprime (1 + a * b) := by\\n    rw [Nat.coprime_add_mul_right_right]\\n    exact Nat.coprime_one_right _\\n\\n  have a_euler := Nat.ModEq.pow_totient a_coprime\\n  have b_euler := Nat.ModEq.pow_totient b_coprime\\n  rw [\u2190ZMod.natCast_eq_natCast_iff] at a_euler b_euler\\n\\n  have totient_pos :=\\n    ((1 + a*b).totient_pos).mpr ((zero_le _).trans_lt (lt_one_add _))\\n\\n  suffices h : 1 + a * b \u2223 g\\n  \xb7 specialize D (\u03c6 (1+a*b)*N) (Nat.le_mul_of_pos_left N totient_pos)\\n    have \u27e8Ha, Hb\u27e9 :=\\n      D \u25b8 Nat.gcd_dvd (a ^ (\u03c6 (1 + a * b) * N) + b) (b ^ (\u03c6 (1 + a * b) * N) + a)\\n    replace Ha := h.trans Ha; replace Hb := h.trans Hb\\n    clear g D a_coprime b_coprime totient_pos h\\n    rw [\u2190ZMod.natCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd, pow_mul] at Ha Hb\\n    push_cast at a_euler b_euler Ha Hb\\n    rw [a_euler] at Ha; clear a_euler\\n    rw [b_euler] at Hb; clear b_euler\\n    rw [one_pow] at Ha Hb\\n    norm_cast at Ha Hb\\n    rw [ZMod.natCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd] at Ha Hb\\n    replace Ha := Nat.le_of_dvd (Nat.add_pos_right 1 b_pos) Ha\\n    replace Hb := Nat.le_of_dvd (Nat.add_pos_right 1 a_pos) Hb\\n    rw [Prod.mk.injEq]\\n    constructor <;> nlinarith only [a_pos, b_pos, Ha, Hb]\\n\\n  suffices h : \u2203 n \u2265 N, 1 + a * b \u2223 a * (a ^ n + b) \u2227\\n                        1 + a * b \u2223 b * (b ^ n + a)\\n  \xb7 obtain \u27e8n, hn, h\u27e9 := h\\n    replace h := And.intro\\n      (Nat.Coprime.dvd_of_dvd_mul_left a_coprime.symm h.1)\\n      (Nat.Coprime.dvd_of_dvd_mul_left b_coprime.symm h.2)\\n    replace h := Nat.dvd_gcd h.1 h.2\\n    rw [D _ hn] at h\\n    exact h\\n  clear D g a_coprime b_coprime a_pos b_pos\\n\\n  use \u03c6 (1 + a * b) * (N + 1) - 1\\n  use N.le_sub_of_add_le (Nat.le_mul_of_pos_left (N + 1) totient_pos)\\n\\n  obtain \u27e8w, h : _ = w + 1\u27e9 := Nat.exists_eq_add_of_le\' totient_pos\\n  clear totient_pos\\n  simp only [\u2190ZMod.natCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd, pow_add]\\n  push_cast at a_euler b_euler \u22a2\\n  rw [h] at a_euler b_euler \u22a2; clear h\\n  constructor <;>\\n  simp only [mul_add, \u2190pow_succ\', mul_one, Nat.add_succ_sub_one] <;>\\n  rw [add_assoc, \u2190mul_add_one] <;>\\n  rw [pow_mul] <;>\\n  simp only [a_euler, b_euler] <;>\\n  clear a_euler b_euler <;>\\n  simp only [one_pow] <;>\\n  norm_cast <;>\\n  simp only [ZMod.natCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd] <;>\\n  ring_nf <;>\\n  exact dvd_refl _\\n```\\n\\n</details>\\n\\nIn 2025, both [DeepMind][deepmind-imo-2025] and [OpenAI][openai-imo-2025] reported gold-medal-level IMO performance, each solving five of the six problems. Unlike AlphaProof\'s 2024 results, these solutions were written in natural language rather than Lean.\\n\\n## Proofs in Lean\\n\\nAt this point we can touch on the core mechanics of actually doing mathematics in Lean. Recall that a theorem in Lean is a _type_, and a proof is obtained by constructing an expression of that type.\\n\\n### Tactics\\n\\nThere are multiple ways to do this in Lean, but the way that most closely resembles mathematics (and thus the most common approach in places like `Mathlib`) is called a **tactic proof**. Tactic proofs begin with the keyword `by` and proceed by applying a sequence of tactics. A tactic is a _meta-program_ that helps automatically construct expressions of the desired type.\\n\\nHere are some basic examples to give the idea.\\n\\n<TacticTable>\\n\\n<TacticRow name=\\"rfl\\">\\n\\nCloses a goal when the two sides of an equality are definitionally equal.\\n\\n```lean\\nexample : 1 = 1 := by\\n  rfl\\n```\\n\\n</TacticRow>\\n\\n<TacticRow name=\\"rw\\">\\n\\n`rw [e]` applies identity `e` as a rewrite rule to the target of the main goal.\\n\\n```lean\\nexample (a b : \u2115) (h : a = b) :\\n    a + 1 = b + 1 := by\\n  rw [h]\\n```\\n\\n</TacticRow>\\n\\n<TacticRow name=\\"exact\\">\\n\\n`exact e` closes the main goal if its target type matches that of `e`.\\n\\n```lean\\nexample (a : \u2115) : a + 0 = a := by\\n  exact Nat.add_zero a\\n```\\n\\n</TacticRow>\\n\\n<TacticRow name=\\"apply\\">\\n\\n`apply e` tries to match the current goal against the conclusion of `e`\'s type.\\n\\n```lean\\nexample (p q : Prop) (h_p : p)\\n    (h_pq : p \u2192 q) : q := by\\n  -- we need to show q\\n  -- apply the hypothesis h_pq,\\n  -- so now we need to show p\\n  apply h_pq\\n  -- but we have p, it\'s exactly\\n  -- the hypothesis h_p!\\n  exact h_p\\n```\\n\\n</TacticRow>\\n\\n<TacticRow name=\\"linarith\\">\\n\\nA powerful tactic that solves linear inequalities of various kinds.\\n\\n```lean\\nexample : (0 : \u211d) \u2264 73 := by\\n  linarith\\n```\\n\\n</TacticRow>\\n\\n<TacticRow name=\\"induction\'\\">\\n\\n`induction\' n with d hd` starts a proof by induction. Here `hd` becomes the induction hypothesis - i.e., we assume the statement holds for `n = d` and need to prove it holds for `n = d + 1`.\\n\\n```lean\\nexample (a : \u2115) : a + 1 = 1 + a := by\\n  -- begin a proof by induction\\n  induction\' a with d hd\\n  -- case a = 0 amounts to proving 1 = 1\\n  \xb7 rfl\\n  -- case a = d, the inductive step\\n  \xb7 linarith\\n```\\n\\n</TacticRow>\\n\\n<TacticRow name=\\"simp\\">\\n\\nUses lemmas and hypotheses to simplify the main goal target or non-dependent hypotheses.\\n\\n```lean\\nexample (a b c d : \u2115) :\\n    (a + 0) * 1 + (b * 0 + c) +\\n      (d + 0) * 1 = a + c + d := by\\n  simp\\n```\\n\\n</TacticRow>\\n\\n</TacticTable>\\n\\nTactics allow us to approach proving statements in Lean in a similar way to proving statements in mathematics. In either setting, one structures their argument as a series of steps until the final goal has been obtained. Tactics are a powerful concept as they let you construct programs to write proofs interactively, procedurally and even automatically, as we will see.\\n\\n### The proof state\\n\\nTactics, it turns out, are only half the story when writing proofs in Lean. The other half is the **proof state**. This is a representation of the current goal you are trying to prove, together with all the local context you have (like hypotheses that are given, or that you have established along the way).\\n\\nLean reports the current proof state in the Lean Infoview window (powered by the Lean LSP in tools like VS Code, for example). A typical view might look something like this:\\n\\n```text\\n1 goal\\nn : \u2115\\nhn : 1 < n\\n\u22a2 \u2203 k, IsPrime k \u2227 k \u2223 n\\n```\\n\\nThis can be read as follows:\\n\\n- There is currently `1 goal`, that is, one thing we need to prove.\\n- We have a natural number `n : \u2115` and a hypothesis `hn : 1 < n` that $n$ is greater than 1.\\n- The thing we need to prove is after the `\u22a2` symbol. In this case we need to prove the existence of a number $k$ that divides $n$ and that is prime.\\n\\nAs you apply tactics, you modify the proof state. You might do this by adding new variables or hypotheses, by rewriting or adjusting the remaining goal, by splitting the existing goal into subgoals, and so forth.\\n\\n### David Renshaw\'s chess analogy\\n\\nDavid Renshaw [draws the following analogy][renshaw-analogy] between chess and Lean.\\n\\n**Chess.** You can write down a chess game as a sequence of moves in [algebraic notation][chess-notation], but this leaves _implicit_ the main visual element of chess: the position of the pieces on the board. For example, here\'s a representation of the [_Game of the Century_][game-of-century].\\n\\n```text\\n1.Nf3 Nf6 2.c4 g6 3.Nc3 Bg7 4.d4 O-O 5.Bf4 d5 6.Qb3 dxc4\\n7.Qxc4 c6 8.e4 Nbd7 9.Rd1 Nb6 10.Qc5 Bg4 11.Bg5 Na4\\n12.Qa3 Nxc3 13.bxc3 Nxe4 14.Bxe7 Qb6 15.Bc4 Nxc3\\n16.Bc5 Rfe8+ 17.Kf1 Be6 18.Bxb6 Bxc4+ 19.Kg1 Ne2+\\n20.Kf1 Nxd4+ 21.Kg1 Ne2+ 22.Kf1 Nc3+ 23.Kg1 axb6\\n24.Qb4 Ra4 25.Qxb6 Nxd1 26.h3 Rxa2 27.Kh2 Nxf2\\n28.Re1 Rxe1 29.Qd8+ Bf8 30.Nxe1 Bd5 31.Nf3 Ne4\\n32.Qb8 b5 33.h4 h5 34.Ne5 Kg7 35.Kg1 Bc5+ 36.Kf1 Ng3+\\n37.Ke1 Bb4+ 38.Kd1 Bb3+ 39.Kc1 Ne2+ 40.Kb1 Nc3+\\n41.Kc1 Rc2# 0-1\\n```\\n\\nWhen we play chess we want to _see_ the board as a way to help reason over the game state.\\n\\n**Lean.** You can write a proof in the Lean theorem prover as a sequence of steps, but this format leaves _implicit_ the main visual element of theorem proving: the goal state. For example, here\'s the statement about prime factors from earlier:\\n\\n```lean\\ntheorem exists_prime_factor :\\n    \u2200 n, 1 < n \u2192 \u2203 k, IsPrime k \u2227 k \u2223 n\\n```\\n\\nWhen we use Lean we want to _see_ the goal we are trying to prove. Lean provides this information, and we can watch it evolve as we make steps in the proof. The walkthrough below follows the complete proof, from the initial goal to both cases being closed.\\n\\n<ProofWalkthrough />\\n\\n### Mathlib, and the growing repository of results\\n\\nAs mentioned above, `Mathlib` is an extensive library of definitions and theorems. As of November 2025, Mathlib had over 240k theorems and over 100k definitions. Lean supports importing and using existing results to help prove theorems. One simply needs to import the relevant module, e.g., `import Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Basic`.\\n\\n### Tactics and automation\\n\\nBeyond the existence of a large repository of known results, Lean has various kinds of automation built in to assist the working mathematician.\\n\\n**Example: Rewrite suggestion.** In Lean there is a rewrite move denoted `rw` which applies a fact to the current proof state to modify the goal. One can interact with Lean by asking `rw?` (with a question mark) to get suggestions based on your active state!\\n\\n```lean\\nimport Mathlib\\n\\nexample (a b c : Nat) : a * b * c = b * (a * c) :=\\nby\\n  rw?\\n```\\n\\nPossible rewrite suggestions from `Mathlib` include:\\n\\n```text\\nTry this: rw [Nat.mul_right_comm]\\n-- a * c * b = b * (a * c)\\nTry this: rw [Nat.mul_assoc]\\n-- a * (b * c) = b * (a * c)\\nTry this: rw [Nat.mul_left_comm]\\n-- a * b * c = a * (b * c)\\nTry this: rw [Nat.mul_comm]\\n-- c * (a * b) = b * (a * c)\\nTry this: rw [\u2190 Nat.mul_assoc]\\n-- a * b * c = b * a * c\\n```\\n\\n## What\'s the difference between formal and informal reasoning, and why should you care?\\n\\nConsider the following example question from AMC-12:\\n\\nLet $x, y \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}$ satisfy $|x + y| + |x-y| = 2$. Show that\\n\\n$$\\nx^2 - 6x + y^2 \\\\leq 8\\n$$\\n\\nHere\'s an excerpt from GPT-5\'s attempt at solving this problem:\\n\\n> The constraint $|x+y|+|x-y|=2$ is the boundary of a diamond (a square rotated $45^\\\\circ$) with vertices $(\\\\pm1,\\\\pm1)$.\\n>\\n> We want to maximize\\n>\\n> $$\\n> f(x,y)=x^2-6x+y^2=(x-3)^2+y^2-9.\\n> $$\\n>\\n> So maximizing $f$ is the same as maximizing the squared distance from $(3,0)$:\\n>\\n> $$\\n> (x-3)^2+y^2.\\n> $$\\n>\\n> On that diamond, the farthest points from $(3,0)$ are the **left** vertices $(-1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$. (Geometrically: draw the diamond and the point $(3,0)$; the farthest boundary points are the two left corners.)\\n>\\n> Evaluate:\\n>\\n> - $f(-1,1)=(-1)^2-6(-1)+1^2=1+6+1=8$.\\n> - $f(-1,-1)=1+6+1=8$.\\n> - For comparison, the right vertices give $f(1,\\\\pm1)=1-6+1=-4$.\\n>\\n> **Answer**\\n>\\n> - **Maximum value:** (8)\\n> - **Where:** at $\\\\boxed{(-1,1)\\\\ \\\\text{and}\\\\ (-1,-1)}$\\n\\nNotice:\\n\\n- GPT-5 makes a geometric argument based on the shape determined by the given constraint.\\n- Its description is **inconsistent**: the vertices $(\\\\pm1,\\\\pm1)$ define an axis-aligned square, not a square rotated by 45 degrees.\\n- It gives no justification for identifying the constraint with that boundary.\\n- Completing the square correctly reduces the problem to maximizing the distance from $(3,0)$.\\n- The final maximizing points are correct, but the geometric explanation is muddled. A correct answer alone does not establish that the reasoning is sound.\\n\\nHere\'s a picture of the region enclosed by the constraint in the problem (shaded in green). The constraint itself is the boundary of that region. Looking at this, it\'s possible to guess at the reason the model mentioned diamonds rotated by 45 degrees.\\n\\n<ConstraintFigure />\\n\\nQuestion: how much credit should be assigned to this answer? It has the right _flavor_, and indeed makes many of the correct steps. But it leaves a gap between a plausible explanation and a justified argument. Worse, that gap is **not obvious at a glance**! This makes an LLM-as-a-judge-based reward hard to trust.\\n\\nConsider now the formal version of this problem:\\n\\n```lean\\nexample\\n  (x y : \u211d)\\n  (h\u2080 : abs (x + y) + abs (x - y) = 2) : x ^ 2 - 6 * x + y ^ 2 \u2264 8\\n```\\n\\nNotice that it is straightforward to give the formal version of this problem in Lean.\\n\\nThe advantage of formalization is that the proof can be checked against a precise statement, rather than relying on a fallible LLM-as-a-judge. We still need to ensure that the formal statement matches the intended problem, that the proof has no gaps, and that it uses only permitted axioms.\\n\\nHere is a model-generated formal solution. Before expanding the argument, here are some key observations:\\n\\n- This argument was model-generated, showing that models are capable of detailed Lean-based proofs.\\n- The proof is over 80 lines long. There is some tax to pay for being precise in one\'s argument.\\n- If you are not familiar with Lean, the argument may be tricky to follow. But remember, Lean is just a programming language (admittedly with a fairly steep learning curve).\\n\\n<details>\\n<summary>Full Lean proof</summary>\\n\\n```lean\\n by\\n  by_cases hxy : x + y \u2265 0\\n  \xb7 by_cases hxx : x - y \u2265 0\\n    \xb7 have hx : x = 1 := by\\n        rw [abs_of_nonneg hxy, abs_of_nonneg hxx] at h\u2080\\n        linarith\\n      have hy_le : y \u2264 1 := by linarith [hx, hxx]\\n      have hy_ge : y \u2265 -1 := by linarith [hx, hxy]\\n      have hy_abs : abs y \u2264 1 := by\\n        apply abs_le.mpr\\n        exact \u27e8hy_ge, hy_le\u27e9\\n      have hy_tmp : (abs y) ^ 2 \u2264 abs y := by\\n        rw [pow_two]\\n        apply mul_le_of_le_one_right\\n        \xb7 simp\\n        \xb7 exact hy_abs\\n      have hy_sq : y ^ 2 \u2264 1 := by\\n        simpa [sq_abs] using le_trans hy_tmp hy_abs\\n      calc\\n        x ^ 2 - 6 * x + y ^ 2 = 1 - 6 + y ^ 2 := by simp [hx]\\n        _ = y ^ 2 - 5 := by ring\\n        _ \u2264 1 - 5 := by linarith [hy_sq]\\n        _ = -4 := by norm_num\\n        _ \u2264 8 := by linarith\\n    \xb7 have hy : y = 1 := by\\n        rw [abs_of_nonneg hxy, abs_of_nonpos (le_of_not_ge hxx)] at h\u2080\\n        linarith\\n      have hx_le : x \u2264 1 := by linarith [hy, hxx]\\n      have hx_ge : x \u2265 -1 := by linarith [hy, hxy]\\n      have hx_abs : abs x \u2264 1 := by\\n        apply abs_le.mpr\\n        exact \u27e8hx_ge, hx_le\u27e9\\n      have hx_tmp : (abs x) ^ 2 \u2264 abs x := by\\n        rw [pow_two]\\n        apply mul_le_of_le_one_right\\n        \xb7 simp\\n        \xb7 exact hx_abs\\n      have hx_sq : x ^ 2 \u2264 1 := by\\n        simpa [sq_abs] using le_trans hx_tmp hx_abs\\n      have h_lin : -6 * x \u2264 6 := by\\n        linarith [hx_ge]\\n      calc\\n        x ^ 2 - 6 * x + y ^ 2 = x ^ 2 - 6 * x + 1 := by simp [hy]\\n        _ \u2264 1 + 6 + 1 := by linarith [hx_sq, h_lin]\\n        _ = 8 := by norm_num\\n  \xb7 by_cases hxx : x - y \u2265 0\\n    \xb7 have hy : y = -1 := by\\n        rw [abs_of_nonpos (le_of_not_ge hxy), abs_of_nonneg hxx] at h\u2080\\n        linarith\\n      have hx_ge : x \u2265 -1 := by linarith [hy, hxx]\\n      have hx_le : x \u2264 1 := by linarith [hy, hxy]\\n      have hx_abs : abs x \u2264 1 := by\\n        apply abs_le.mpr\\n        exact \u27e8hx_ge, hx_le\u27e9\\n      have hx_tmp : (abs x) ^ 2 \u2264 abs x := by\\n        rw [pow_two]\\n        apply mul_le_of_le_one_right\\n        \xb7 simp\\n        \xb7 exact hx_abs\\n      have hx_sq : x ^ 2 \u2264 1 := by\\n        simpa [sq_abs] using le_trans hx_tmp hx_abs\\n      have h_lin : -6 * x \u2264 6 := by\\n        linarith [hx_ge]\\n      calc\\n        x ^ 2 - 6 * x + y ^ 2 = x ^ 2 - 6 * x + 1 := by simp [hy]\\n        _ \u2264 1 + 6 + 1 := by linarith [hx_sq, h_lin]\\n        _ = 8 := by norm_num\\n    \xb7 have hx : x = -1 := by\\n        rw [abs_of_nonpos (le_of_not_ge hxy), abs_of_nonpos (le_of_not_ge hxx)] at h\u2080\\n        linarith\\n      have hy_le : y \u2264 1 := by linarith [hx, hxy]\\n      have hy_ge : y \u2265 -1 := by linarith [hx, hxx]\\n      have hy_abs : abs y \u2264 1 := by\\n        apply abs_le.mpr\\n        exact \u27e8hy_ge, hy_le\u27e9\\n      have hy_tmp : (abs y) ^ 2 \u2264 abs y := by\\n        rw [pow_two]\\n        apply mul_le_of_le_one_right\\n        \xb7 simp\\n        \xb7 exact hy_abs\\n      have hy_sq : y ^ 2 \u2264 1 := by\\n        simpa [sq_abs] using le_trans hy_tmp hy_abs\\n      calc\\n        x ^ 2 - 6 * x + y ^ 2 = 1 + 6 + y ^ 2 := by simp [hx]\\n        _ = 7 + y ^ 2 := by norm_num\\n        _ \u2264 7 + 1 := by linarith [hy_sq]\\n        _ = 8 := by norm_num\\n```\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n**Takeaways.**\\n\\nThis example highlights fundamental challenges with informal reasoning-based methods stemming from the difficulty with scalably identifying flawed reasoning trajectories, particularly in cases where the flaws are subtle. We see how formally verifiable solutions provide a remedy.\\n\\n[lean-docs]: https://leanprover-community.github.io/\\n[future-of-mathematics]: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Dp-mQ3HxgDE\\n[xena-intro]: https://xenaproject.wordpress.com/2021/01/21/formalising-mathematics-an-introduction/\\n[imperial-math-repo]: https://github.com/ImperialCollegeLondon/formalising-mathematics-2024\\n[liquid-tensor]: https://xenaproject.wordpress.com/2020/12/05/liquid-tensor-experiment/\\n[liquid-pdf]: https://www.ma.imperial.ac.uk/~buzzard/xena/pdfs/liquid_tensor_experiment.pdf\\n[liquid-6mo]: https://xenaproject.wordpress.com/2021/06/05/half-a-year-of-the-liquid-tensor-experiment-amazing-developments/\\n[liquid-beyond]: https://xenaproject.wordpress.com/2022/09/12/beyond-the-liquid-tensor-experiment/\\n[liquid-deps]: https://leanprover-community.github.io/liquid/dep_graph_section_1.html\\n[tao-talk]: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_sTDSO74D8Q\\n[flt-docs]: https://lean-lang.org/use-cases/flt/\\n[deepmind]: https://deepmind.google/blog/ai-solves-imo-problems-at-silver-medal-level/\\n[deepmind-imo-2025]: https://deepmind.google/blog/advanced-version-of-gemini-with-deep-think-officially-achieves-gold-medal-standard-at-the-international-mathematical-olympiad/\\n[openai-imo-2025]: https://x.com/alexwei_/status/1946477742855532918\\n[alpha-proof-imo-24-p2]: https://gist.github.com/dwrensha/68de74feb8f0b0700ba5454ae056a191\\n[mathlib]: https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4\\n[mathlib-white-paper]: https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.09336\\n[mathlib-growth-paper]: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.21593v2\\n[mathlib-stats]: https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib_stats.html\\n[renshaw-analogy]: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KuxFWwwlEtc\\n[chess-notation]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_notation_(chess)\\n[game-of-century]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_of_the_Century_(chess)"},{"id":"composition-factors","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/composition-factors","source":"@site/blog/2018-09-01-composition-factors.mdx","title":"Composition factors and representation stability","description":"Computing Schur-functor multiplicities by decomposition puzzles and shape analysis, and the representation stability revealed by the resulting coefficients.","date":"2018-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Mathematics","permalink":"/blog/tags/mathematics"},{"inline":false,"label":"Algorithms","permalink":"/blog/tags/algorithms"}],"readingTime":14.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Amin Saied","title":"Member of Technical Staff, Microsoft AI","url":"https://github.com/aminsaied","imageURL":"https://github.com/aminsaied.png","key":"aminsaied","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Composition factors and representation stability","date":"2018-09-01T00:00:00.000Z","description":"Computing Schur-functor multiplicities by decomposition puzzles and shape analysis, and the representation stability revealed by the resulting coefficients.","slug":"composition-factors","authors":["aminsaied"],"tags":["mathematics","algorithms"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Lean, formal proofs, and verifiable reasoning","permalink":"/blog/lean-formal-proofs"}},"content":"import {\\n  CompositionProvider,\\n  DegreeFiveMatrix,\\n  DecompositionPuzzleCartoon,\\n  CoefficientAtlas,\\n  ShapeAnalysis,\\n  StabilityExplorer,\\n  DataDownload,\\n} from \'@site/src/components/CompositionFactors\';\\n\\nIn 2017, my advisor asked me to compute a family of coefficients\\n$c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}$ arising in the study of the Johnson homomorphism of the\\nmapping class group.\\n\\nTheir connection with mapping class groups is not essential to this story.\\nInstead, I want to describe how to compute them efficiently, and how\\nexpanding the range of computation revealed hints of a deeper structure\\namong the coefficients.[^thesis]\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<CompositionProvider>\\n\\n## Plethysms afoot\\n\\nSo, briefly, what are the coefficients $c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}$?\\n\\nRecall that, given a partition $\\\\lambda\\\\vdash n$, the **Schur functor**\\n$\\\\mathbb S_\\\\lambda$ assigns to a vector space $V$ the image of a Young symmetrizer acting on\\n$V^{\\\\otimes n}$. Two familiar examples are the symmetric powers\\n$\\\\mathbb S_{(n)}(V)=\\\\operatorname{Sym}^n(V)$ and exterior powers\\n$\\\\mathbb S_{(1^n)}(V)=\\\\bigwedge^n V$.\\n\\nThe **free Lie algebra** $\\\\mathcal L(V)$ is the Lie algebra generated by\\n$V$, subject only to antisymmetry and the Jacobi identity. It has a natural\\ngrading\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\mathcal L(V)=\\\\bigoplus_{n\\\\geq 1}\\\\mathcal L_n(V),\\n$$\\n\\nwhere $\\\\mathcal L_n(V)$ consists of Lie expressions involving exactly\\n$n$ inputs from $V$.\\n\\nWe are interested in the coefficients $c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}$ defined by the\\ndecomposition\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\mathbb S_\\\\mu\\\\bigl(\\\\mathcal L(V)\\\\bigr)\\n\\\\cong\\n\\\\bigoplus_\\\\lambda\\n\\\\mathbb S_\\\\lambda(V)^{\\\\oplus c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}}.\\n$$\\n\\nIn other words, $c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}$ is the multiplicity of\\n$\\\\mathbb S_\\\\lambda(V)$ in $\\\\mathbb S_\\\\mu(\\\\mathcal L(V))$.[^conventions]\\n\\nHow might we compute these coefficients?\\n\\nThe first observation is that the graded pieces of the free Lie algebra\\nthemselves admit Schur-functor decompositions:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\mathcal L_n(V)\\\\cong\\n\\\\bigoplus_{\\\\nu\\\\vdash n}\\\\mathbb S_\\\\nu(V)^{\\\\oplus m_\\\\nu}.\\n$$\\n\\nMoreover, the multiplicities $m_\\\\nu$ are well understood. They can be\\ncomputed using symmetric-group characters, or by counting certain\\nstandard Young tableaux.[^lie-multiplicities]\\n\\nSo we know how to decompose $\\\\mathcal L(V)$. The difficulty comes when\\nwe apply another Schur functor $\\\\mathbb S_\\\\mu$ to it.\\n\\nThis leads us directly to **plethysm**.\\n\\nPlethysm describes the composition of Schur functors. Given partitions\\n$\\\\mu$ and $\\\\nu$, we seek the decomposition\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\mathbb S_\\\\mu\\\\bigl(\\\\mathbb S_\\\\nu(V)\\\\bigr)\\n\\\\cong\\n\\\\bigoplus_{\\\\lambda\\\\vdash|\\\\mu||\\\\nu|}\\n\\\\mathbb S_\\\\lambda(V)^{\\\\oplus p_{\\\\mu,\\\\nu}^{\\\\lambda}}.\\n$$\\n\\nThe nonnegative integers $p_{\\\\mu,\\\\nu}^{\\\\lambda}$ are the **plethysm\\ncoefficients**. In the language of symmetric functions, writing\\n$s_\\\\lambda$ for the Schur function associated with $\\\\mathbb S_\\\\lambda$,\\nthe same calculation is\\n\\n$$\\ns_\\\\mu[s_\\\\nu]=\\\\sum_\\\\lambda p_{\\\\mu,\\\\nu}^{\\\\lambda}s_\\\\lambda.\\n$$\\n\\nFor our problem, let $\\\\mathrm{Lie}_n=\\\\sum_{\\\\nu\\\\vdash n}m_\\\\nu s_\\\\nu$\\nencode the degree-$n$ piece of the free Lie algebra. The coefficients\\nwe seek are precisely those in the expansion:[^lie-series]\\n\\n$$\\ns_\\\\mu\\\\left[\\\\sum_{n\\\\geq 1}\\\\mathrm{Lie}_n\\\\right]\\n=\\\\sum_\\\\lambda c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}s_\\\\lambda.\\n$$\\n\\nThus the representation-theoretic question reduces to computing plethysms\\nof symmetric functions.\\n\\nUnfortunately, **plethysm is notoriously difficult**.[^plethysm-complexity] Littlewood-Richardson\\ncoefficients, which govern tensor products of Schur functors, admit an\\nelegant positive combinatorial rule. No comparably general positive rule\\nis known for plethysm coefficients.[^plethysm-combinatorics] Even determining which irreducible\\nrepresentations occur in a plethysm can be subtle, and general\\ncomputations quickly become prohibitively expensive as the partitions grow.\\n\\n## Roll up your sleeves\\n\\nSmall examples of the coefficients $c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}$ can be computed by\\nhand, but the calculations quickly become intractable. Sooner or later,\\none has to turn to a computer.\\n\\nA confession: this was the first time in my mathematical career that I\\nhad found myself turning to a computer for help. My first attempts were\\nbrute force and, unsurprisingly, rather inefficient. They only produced\\ncoefficients through degree 5.\\n\\nThere are 18 nonempty partitions through degree 5, giving the\\n$18\\\\times 18$ matrix below. Rows index the target partition $\\\\lambda$\\nand columns the source partition $\\\\mu$.\\n\\n<DegreeFiveMatrix />\\n\\nTwo features are immediately clear: the diagonal consists entirely of\\n1s, and everything above it is zero. Both are simple to prove directly.\\nSince $\\\\mathcal L_1(V)=V$ and there are no degree-zero terms, the\\nlowest-degree part of $\\\\mathbb S_\\\\mu(\\\\mathcal L(V))$ is\\n$\\\\mathbb S_\\\\mu(V)$ itself, in degree $|\\\\mu|$.\\n\\nWhat interests us, then, is what lies **strictly below the diagonal**.\\nThrough degree 5, there is little to suggest a deeper pattern.\\n\\nThe key insight to improve on the naive brute-force approach was to recast\\nthe coefficients $c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}$ as weighted counts of solutions to\\ncertain combinatorial arrangements I called **decomposition puzzles**.\\nThe underlying combinatorics of these puzzles imposed clear restrictions\\non the kinds of assemblies that are valid, allowing us to significantly\\nreduce the search space.\\n\\n<DecompositionPuzzleCartoon>\\n\\nThe pairing from [Example 3.18 of the paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.04326#page=10)\\ncontributes 1 to $c_{(3,2),(2,1)}$.\\n\\n</DecompositionPuzzleCartoon>\\n\\nAt the level of **degrees**, plethysm becomes multiplication and tensor\\nproduct becomes addition. In the example above, the chosen pairing has\\ndegree $2\\\\times 2+1\\\\times 1=5$. Swapping the Lie pieces gives\\n$2\\\\times 1+1\\\\times 2=4$, immediately ruling out that pairing for a\\ndegree-5 target.\\n\\nThis cheap check is the starting point of **shape analysis**.[^shape-arithmetic]\\nBy grouping decompositions with the same part sizes, I could perform the\\nsearch once per shape and reuse the resulting instructions across many\\ncalculations. I will give the details below.\\n\\nThis was the algorithm described in my\\n[2017 paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.04326). It extended the computation\\nto degree 14: a $507\\\\times 507$ matrix containing **257,049 entries**,\\ncompared with the original 324.\\n\\nVisualizing these expanded coefficients revealed a striking block-stability\\npattern that immediately brought to mind Church, Ellenberg, and Farb\'s\\nwork on FI-modules.[^fi-modules] Blocks in one part of the matrix seemed\\nto recur, with changes, farther down and to the right.\\n\\nYou can explore this observation yourself in the interactive figure below:\\ntoggle **Show degree boundaries** to separate the matrix into blocks of\\nfixed source and target degree.\\nUse the maximum-degree slider to compare the expanded table with the\\ndegree-5 calculation.\\n\\n<CoefficientAtlas />\\n\\nBefore examining that pattern, let us look more closely at the puzzles\\nthat made a computation of this size possible.\\n\\n## Decomposition puzzles\\n\\nReturn to the decomposition of the free Lie algebra, now indexing its\\nirreducible summands individually:\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\mathcal L(V)\\\\cong\\n\\\\bigoplus_{i\\\\geq 1}\\\\mathbb S_{\\\\ell_i}(V).\\n$$\\n\\nThe partitions $\\\\ell_i$ label **Lie pieces**, with repeated summands\\nlisted separately. A $\\\\mu$-decomposition is a tuple\\n$D=(\\\\mu_1,\\\\ldots,\\\\mu_k)$ of nonempty partitions satisfying\\n$\\\\sum_j|\\\\mu_j|=|\\\\mu|$. Its iterated Littlewood-Richardson coefficient is\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\alpha_\\\\mu(D)\\n=[s_\\\\mu]\\\\bigl(s_{\\\\mu_1}\\\\cdots s_{\\\\mu_k}\\\\bigr).\\n$$\\n\\nRetain only the decompositions with $\\\\alpha_\\\\mu(D)>0$. For each one,\\nchoose a tuple $I=(i_1,\\\\ldots,i_k)$ of distinct Lie-piece indices and\\nform the **assembly**\\n\\n$$\\nA(D,I)=\\\\prod_{j=1}^k s_{\\\\mu_j}[s_{\\\\ell_{i_j}}],\\n$$\\n\\nwhere brackets denote plethysm. The multiplicity of a target partition\\n$\\\\lambda$ in this assembly is\\n$\\\\beta_\\\\lambda(D,I)=[s_\\\\lambda]A(D,I)$.\\n\\nA pairing in which $\\\\lambda$ occurs is a **solution** to the puzzle,\\nwith weight $\\\\alpha_\\\\mu(D)\\\\beta_\\\\lambda(D,I)$. Theorem 3.21 of the\\npaper identifies $c_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}$ with the sum over the set\\n$\\\\Sigma(\\\\mu,\\\\lambda)$ of distinct solutions:[^puzzle-weights]\\n\\n$$\\nc_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu}\\n=\\\\sum_{\\\\sigma\\\\in\\\\Sigma(\\\\mu,\\\\lambda)}\\n\\\\alpha_\\\\mu(\\\\sigma)\\\\,\\\\beta_\\\\lambda(\\\\sigma).\\n$$\\n\\n## Shape analysis\\n\\nEnumerating every assembly would still require too many expensive\\nplethysms. The useful restriction comes from homogeneity: an assembly has\\ndegree $\\\\sum_j|\\\\mu_j|\\\\,|\\\\ell_{i_j}|$, so it can contribute to\\n$\\\\lambda\\\\vdash d$ only when that sum equals $d$.\\n\\nThis condition depends on the decomposition only through its **shape**\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\theta=(|\\\\mu_1|,\\\\ldots,|\\\\mu_k|),\\n$$\\n\\nwith the pieces ordered by decreasing size. Writing $r_i=|\\\\ell_i|$\\nfor the Lie-piece degrees, the condition becomes\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\theta_1r_{i_1}+\\\\cdots+\\\\theta_kr_{i_k}=d.\\n$$\\n\\nThe shape also gives an upper bound on the degrees of the Lie pieces\\nwe need to consider.[^degree-bound] The algorithm searches this smaller\\ncollection for degree-compatible pairings and caches their indices.\\nThese **instructions** can then be reused by every decomposition of\\nthe same shape.\\n\\n<ShapeAnalysis />\\n\\nFor $\\\\theta=(2,1)$ and target degree 5, the ten available Lie pieces\\ngive 90 ordered pairs. The bound restricts us to pieces of degree at\\nmost 3, leaving six pairs. The equation $2r_{i_1}+r_{i_2}=5$ then leaves\\njust two: the degree pairs $(1,3)$ and $(2,1)$.\\n\\nThe instruction count is not itself a coefficient. For\\n$\\\\mu=(2,1)$ and $D=((2),(1))$, each of these two assemblies contributes\\none copy of the target $(3,2)$. The decomposition\\n$D\'=((1,1),(1))$ contributes one further copy, so the full weighted sum is\\n\\n$$\\nc_{(3,2),(2,1)}=3.\\n$$\\n\\n## The algorithm and its computational range\\n\\nFor a fixed target degree $d$, the implementation computes all coefficients\\nwith $|\\\\lambda|=d$ simultaneously:\\n\\n1. Construct the instruction lookup for each shape and target degree.\\n2. Enumerate the decompositions with nonzero Littlewood-Richardson\\n   multiplicity.\\n3. Assemble the compatible instructions and accumulate their Schur\\n   coefficients with the appropriate weights and overcount correction.\\n\\nThe optimization does not eliminate plethysm; it avoids evaluating\\nassemblies that cannot contribute.\\n\\nThe following comparison is from the experiments reported in the paper:\\n\\n| Quantity | Direct baseline | Decomposition-puzzle algorithm |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Largest degree reached in the paper | 5 | 14 |\\n| Entries in the resulting full matrix | 324 | 257,049 |\\n| Time to compute through degree 5 | 119 seconds | 0.239 seconds |\\n\\nThe paper\'s **\\"over 750-fold\\"** claim refers to the expanded number of\\nmatrix entries. The degree-5 timing comparison is a separate result:\\nroughly 498 times faster in that experiment.\\n\\nThese are timings from the 2017 experiments, not benchmarks of current\\nversions of Sage.[^timings]\\n\\n## Diagonal stabilization\\n\\nReturn now to the pattern in the expanded matrix. The similarities\\nbetween successive $(m,d)$- and $(m+1,d+1)$-blocks, where $m$ and $d$\\nare the source and target degrees, suggest adding a box\\nto the first row of each indexing partition. Both degrees increase by\\none, while their difference stays fixed.\\n\\nWrite $\\\\lambda^{+r}=(\\\\lambda_1+r,\\\\lambda_2,\\\\ldots)$ for adding $r$ boxes\\nto the first row. For a fixed pair $(\\\\lambda,\\\\mu)$, consider the sequence\\n\\n$$\\nc_{\\\\lambda\\\\mu},\\\\quad\\nc_{\\\\lambda^{+1}\\\\mu^{+1}},\\\\quad\\nc_{\\\\lambda^{+2}\\\\mu^{+2}},\\\\quad\\\\ldots\\n$$\\n\\nFor example, the pair $\\\\lambda=(2,2,1)$, $\\\\mu=(1,1)$ gives the initial\\nvalues $2,11,14$, followed by the value 14 through $r=9$, the endpoint\\nof the degree-14 computation. The figure allows this sequence to be\\ncompared with other pairs, including any pair selected in the matrix.\\n\\n<StabilityExplorer />\\n\\nThe computed plateaus suggest eventual constancy along these diagonals,\\nrather than merely a similarity between finite blocks.\\n\\nConjecture 5.4 of the paper states that, for every fixed pair\\n$\\\\lambda,\\\\mu$, there exist constants $N,C$ such that\\n\\n$$\\nc_{\\\\lambda^{+r}\\\\mu^{+r}}=C\\n\\\\qquad\\\\text{for all }r\\\\geq N.\\n$$\\n\\nThe finite computation motivates this statement but does not prove it or\\ndetermine the onset of permanent stabilization.\\n\\n## Representation stability via PD-modules\\n\\nChapter 5 of the thesis introduces **PD-modules** to describe this\\nsimultaneous growth. They organize representations indexed by two finite\\nsets, with compatible maps that enlarge both sets by the same\\namount.[^pd-modules] Schur-Weyl duality identifies our coefficients as\\nmultiplicities in a PD-module built from the free Lie algebra.\\n\\nThe key condition is **finite generation in rank**: for each fixed\\ndifference between the two degrees, finitely many generators suffice\\nunder these diagonal structure maps. Theorem 5.5.3 shows that PD-modules\\nwith this property satisfy representation stability.\\n\\nFor the free Lie algebra, finite generation comes from the same degree\\nconstraint used by the algorithm. At fixed rank\\n$q=|\\\\lambda|-|\\\\mu|$, it gives\\n\\n$$\\nq=\\\\sum_j|\\\\mu_j|\\\\bigl(|\\\\ell_{i_j}|-1\\\\bigr).\\n$$\\n\\nEvery Lie piece of degree greater than one contributes at least\\n$|\\\\mu_j|$ to this sum. The total source degree assigned to such pieces\\nis therefore at most $q$. All unbounded growth must occur in the factor\\npaired with the unique degree-one Lie piece, $\\\\mathcal L_1(V)=V$.\\nThis growth is encoded by the PD structure maps; the remaining\\nassemblies have bounded degree and supply finitely many generators.\\nTheorem 5.6.4 makes this argument precise.\\n\\nIt follows that our coefficients stabilize under simultaneous first-row\\naugmentation. This is **Theorem 5.6.5** of the thesis, proving the\\nconjecture above.\\n\\nThe computation supplied evidence for the stability statement. The\\nPD-module structure explains why it holds beyond the computed range.\\n\\n## Sources and reproducibility\\n\\nThe article follows\\n[An efficient algorithm computing composition factors of $T(V)^{\\\\otimes n}$](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.04326),\\nespecially Sections 2.3, 3.4, 5, and 6. The proof of stability is in\\n[Chapter 5 of my thesis](https://ecommons.cornell.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/c749bfe3-9841-4770-988b-96aff34fa987/content#page=132);\\nChapter 4 develops the algorithm and its visualizations. The original\\n[Sage implementation is on GitHub](https://github.com/aminsaied/composition_factors/tree/524d90f982ce447b3ddaec0f53409e31d9de5872).\\n\\n**Data provenance.** The figures use freshly reconstructed coefficients,\\nnot values extracted from images or a recovered archive of the original\\nrun. They were computed from the defining plethysm using exact\\narithmetic, independently of the paper\'s puzzle algorithm.[^reconstruction]\\nThe download includes the partition order, source reference, and a\\nmatrix checksum. All interactions use this precomputed table; no algebra\\nis evaluated in the browser.\\n\\n<DataDownload />\\n\\n</CompositionProvider>\\n\\n[^thesis]: Unpacking that structure turned out to be at the heart of my thesis.\\n\\n[^conventions]: Work over a field $\\\\mathbf{k}$ of characteristic zero.\\n    These are universal Schur-functor multiplicities: interpret the identities\\n    degree by degree, or evaluate on $V$ of sufficiently large dimension in\\n    each degree. For nonempty $\\\\mu$, the decomposition extends over\\n    arbitrarily large degrees, but only finitely many partitions $\\\\lambda$\\n    contribute in any given degree.\\n\\n[^lie-multiplicities]: For $\\\\nu\\\\vdash n$, the multiplicity $m_\\\\nu$ counts\\n    standard Young tableaux of shape $\\\\nu$ whose major index is congruent\\n    to 1 modulo $n$. The major index is the sum of entries $i$ for which\\n    $i+1$ appears in a lower row. See\\n    [Theorem 3.4 of the paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.04326#page=6),\\n    following Stanley\'s *Enumerative Combinatorics*, Volume 2.\\n\\n[^lie-series]: Explicitly,\\n\\n    $$\\n    \\\\mathrm{Lie}_n=\\\\frac{1}{n}\\\\sum_{d\\\\mid n}\\n    \\\\mu_{\\\\mathrm{Mob}}(d)\\\\,p_d^{n/d},\\n    $$\\n\\n    where $\\\\mu_{\\\\mathrm{Mob}}$ is the Mobius function and $p_d$ is the\\n    degree-$d$ power-sum symmetric function. The infinite sum is taken in\\n    the degree-completed ring of symmetric functions; computing through\\n    any fixed degree requires only finitely many homogeneous terms.\\n\\n[^plethysm-complexity]: Nick Fischer and Christian Ikenmeyer,\\n    [*The Computational Complexity of Plethysm Coefficients*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.00788),\\n    *Computational Complexity* 29 (2020), Article 8, prove that computing\\n    plethysm coefficients is #P-hard and deciding positivity is NP-hard.\\n    Both results already hold for the symmetric-power family\\n    $s_{(n)}[s_{(m)}]$ with any fixed $m\\\\geq 3$. In this same family,\\n    fixing the outer parameter $n$ permits polynomial-time computation;\\n    see Theorems 3.1-3.2 and Proposition 3.3.\\n\\n[^plethysm-combinatorics]: Richard P. Stanley poses the combinatorial\\n    interpretation problem as Problem 9 in\\n    [*Positivity Problems and Conjectures in Algebraic Combinatorics*](https://math.mit.edu/~rstan/pubs/pubfiles/116.pdf),\\n    in *Mathematics: Frontiers and Perspectives* (AMS, 2000), pp. 295-319.\\n    For a modern account of the open problem and computational techniques,\\n    see Laura Colmenarejo, Rosa Orellana, Franco Saliola, Anne Schilling,\\n    and Mike Zabrocki,\\n    [*The Mystery of Plethysm Coefficients*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07258),\\n    in *Open Problems in Algebraic Combinatorics*, *Proceedings of Symposia\\n    in Pure Mathematics* 110 (2024), pp. 275-292, especially Problem 1.1.\\n\\n[^fi-modules]: Thomas Church, Jordan S. Ellenberg, and Benson Farb,\\n    [*FI-modules and stability for representations of symmetric groups*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.4533),\\n    *Duke Mathematical Journal* 164, no. 9 (2015), pp. 1833-1910.\\n\\n[^puzzle-weights]: The weighted formula follows by iterating the\\n    Littlewood-Richardson rule for Schur functors of direct sums.\\n    Distinct solutions are taken modulo simultaneous permutation of the\\n    paired factors. The implementation fixes a representative decomposition\\n    and enumerates ordered instructions. If its distinct partitions occur\\n    $n_1,\\\\ldots,n_t$ times, it corrects the overcount by\\n    $1/(n_1!\\\\cdots n_t!)$. Repeated Lie pieces at distinct indices remain\\n    distinct summands.\\n\\n[^shape-arithmetic]: See [Proposition 3.24](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.04326#page=12)\\n    and [Figure 6](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1711.04326#page=13) of the paper.\\n    Matching the target degree is a necessary condition; the subsequent\\n    assembly determines which target partitions occur and with what multiplicity.\\n\\n[^degree-bound]: Order the shape parts decreasingly and the Lie-piece\\n    degrees $r_1,r_2,\\\\ldots$ increasingly, counting multiplicities.\\n    Lemma 3.28 of the paper gives the upper bound\\n\\n    $$\\n    \\\\varphi(\\\\theta,d)=\\n    \\\\left\\\\lfloor\\n    \\\\frac{d-\\\\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\\\\theta_jr_j}{\\\\theta_k}\\n    \\\\right\\\\rfloor.\\n    $$\\n\\n    Pairing the largest shape parts with the smallest available degrees\\n    leaves the greatest possible allowance for the remaining piece.\\n\\n[^timings]: Averages of ten runs on a 2.3 GHz Intel Core i7 with 16 GB\\n    of RAM, as reported in the paper.\\n\\n[^pd-modules]: Formally, a PD-module is a functor\\n    $\\\\mathsf{PD}\\\\to\\\\operatorname{Vect}_{\\\\mathbf{k}}$. A morphism between\\n    pairs of finite sets consists of an injection in each coordinate and\\n    a bijection between their complements. Its values in bidegree $(i,n)$\\n    are $(S_i,S_n)$-bimodules; the structure maps preserve the rank $n-i$.\\n    Sections 5.5 and 5.6 of the thesis give the stability theorem and the\\n    free-Lie construction.\\n\\n[^reconstruction]: The exporter, `scripts/generate-composition-data.py`,\\n    uses degree-truncated power-sum plethysm with exact rational arithmetic.\\n    It checks character orthogonality, integrality, nonnegativity, the\\n    equal-degree identity, and examples from the original code. This is\\n    not a port or timing comparison of the original algorithm."}]}}')}}]);